    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Junior Caminero** (74.76) and **Yordan Alvarez** (74.67) leading the charge as our most potent offensive threats. **Caminero** draws a favorable matchup against Merrill Kelly, where a generous SP xERA of 4.85 and a significant +19% boost for SP skill alignment elevate his ceiling, while **Alvarez** remains an auto-start despite the presence of Jack Flaherty, fueled by an elite 0.444 xwOBA and a solidified spot in the two-hole. **Trea Turner** (69.55) and **Kyle Schwarber** (68.77) also project as high-impact assets against C. P\u00e9rez; while the park factor is slightly suppressive, the platoon advantage for both, combined with **Turner**\u2019s elite sprint speed potential and the 5x5 scarcity of stolen bases, makes them essential pillars for today\u2019s production.\n\nThe most difficult decision for the rotation occurred at the utility spot, where **Chase Meidroth** (44.76) narrowly missed the cut. While **Meidroth** demonstrated solid base efficiency, the optimizer prioritized the platoon-heavy upside of **Lars Nootbaar** (58.33) and the high-leverage speed profile of **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (67.08). **Crow-Armstrong**\u2019s score is bolstered by a +14% dynamic platoon boost and a specific situational edge\u2014the opposing pitcher\u2019s delivery cadence creates a prime environment for him to exploit the base paths, a critical factor for our stolen base targets. **Christian Yelich** (56.90) remains in the lineup despite a sub-optimal platoon split against Ryan Rolison, as his high floor compensates for the lack of better bench alternatives. \n\nManagers should remain alert for final lineup confirmations, as **Gary Sanchez**, **Caleb Durbin**, **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, **Nelson Velazquez**, **Lars Nootbaar**, and **Yordan Alvarez** are all currently projected based on historical batting orders. Risk is present for **Colt Keith** (52.29) and **Dillon Dingler** (benched, 37.30), as both face Hunter Brown, an arm that significantly drags down our SP-skill efficiency metrics. We are bypassing **Carson Benge** (39.35) and **Casey Schmitt** (36.77) today, as their projections fell below our strict 40.0 efficiency floor; **Schmitt**, despite the wind advantage, was squeezed out by the sheer talent gap against a high-tier arm like Chris Sale.",
